      "id": "scmst00nnbx17",
      "title": "Integral of the bell curve",
      "createdAt": 1790496384000,
      "updatedAt": 1790496510886,
      "messages": [
        {
          "role": "user",
          "content": "how do we know the integral of the normal density is 1 if there's no antiderivative",
          "ts": 1790496412278
        },
        {
          "role": "assistant",
          "content": "Neat trick (Gauss/Poisson): square it.\n\nI² = ∫∫ e^(-(x²+y²)/2) dx dy over the plane. Switch to polar coordinates: x²+y² = r², dx dy = r dr dθ. Now the r factor gives you an antiderivative!\n\nI² = ∫₀^{2π} ∫₀^∞ e^(-r²/2) r dr dθ = 2π · 1, so I = √(2π). Dividing by √(2π) in φ(x) normalises it to 1.\n\nNot in your notes — it's the classic argument from Analisi 2.",
          "ts": 1790496510886
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
